<p>Ordinary tourists in Paris may make a beeline for Notre-Dame, but they’re hardly likely to find their way out to the other medieval cathedrals in Saint-Denis, north of the city, much less all the way out in Laon. Though glorious in their own way, they’re perhaps even more valuable in providing the context&nbsp;of Gothic church architecture in which Notre-Dame made such a mark.</p>
<p>As Bravo explains it, Notre-Dame’s façade represents “the moment in which Gothic façade achieves its most balanced synthesis. The experiments and innovations of Saint-Denis and Laon are absorbed, disciplined, and unified into a composition in which no element appears arbitrary or independent from the whole.” Among its most influential appreciators was Victor Hugo, in whose time much of Paris’ Gothic architecture was being demolished. His romantic evocations of the medieval built environment in <em>Notre-Dame de Paris</em>&nbsp;(in English, <a href="https://link.amazon/B09LwxgEJ"><em>The Hunchback of Notre-Dame</em></a>) helped save the much deteriorated titular structure, which received an ambitious renovation under <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eug%C3%A8ne_Viollet-le-Duc">Eugène Viollet-le-Duc</a> between 1844 and 1864. That project went beyond period accuracy, adding such non-original details as the gallery of chimeras perched high enough to remain visible during the latest restoration work. Visitors today may notice that the interior doesn’t look exactly like it used to, but some will appreciate that even a monument like Notre-Dame must change with the centuries.</p>
